<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-vectors-linear-combinations">
<title>Determining if a vector is a linear combination of others</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	determine whether the vector <m>\uvec{w}</m>
	can be expressed as a <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-linear-combination" text="custom">linear combination</xref></term>
	of the vectors <m> \uvec{v}_1, \uvec{v}_2, \dotsc, \uvec{v}_m </m>.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{w}   = ( 4,  7,  9) </m>;
		<m> \uvec{v}_1 = ( 0, -2, -1) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_2 = (-1,  3,  0) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_3 = ( 2,  7,  6) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p> Yes, <md> \uvec{w} = -3 \uvec{v}_1 - 2 \uvec{v}_2 + \uvec{v}_3 </md>. </p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{w}   = ( 2,  3,  2) </m>;
		<m> \uvec{v}_1 = ( 1,  1,  1) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_2 = ( 0,  1, -1) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_3 = ( 3,  4,  2) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p> No. </p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{w}   = (-1,  1, -3) </m>;
		<m> \uvec{v}_1 = ( 1,  1,  1) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_2 = ( 0,  1, -1) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_3 = ( 3,  4,  2) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		Yes, there are an infinite number of ways of expressing <m> \uvec{w} </m> as a linear combination of <m> \uvec{v}_1, \uvec{v}_2, \uvec{v}_3 </m>.
		The <q>simplest</q> is <md> \uvec{w} = -7 \uvec{v}_1 + 0 \uvec{v}_2 + 2 \uvec{v}_3 = 2 \uvec{v}_3 - 7 \uvec{v}_1 </md>.
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{w}   = ( 6,  9,  3, 11) </m>;
		<m> \uvec{v}_1 = ( 1,  2,  1,  2) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_2 = ( 4, -1, -2,  3) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v}_3 = ( 5, -4, -4,  2) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p> Yes, <md> \uvec{w} = \uvec{v}_1 + 5 \uvec{v}_2 - 3 \uvec{v}_3 </md>. </p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
